.\" Manual page for the Vox compiler.
.\" Written in groff man macros (not mdoc) so every man implementation
.\" renders it -- see docs/plans/313_man_page.md.
.\" Keep the version in the .TH line in step with Cargo.toml on release.
.TH VOX 1 "2026-08-24" "vox 0.4.12" "Vox Compiler Manual"
.
.SH NAME
vox \- compile English sentences to native x86_64 executables
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vox
.I source.vox
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B vox
.IR source.vox " [" source.vox " ...]"
.B \-\-shared
.RB [ \-o
.IR libname.so ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vox
compiles a constrained, sentence\-based English grammar directly to native
x86\-64 NASM assembly, assembles it with
.BR nasm (1)
and links it with
.BR ld (1).
A program is written as English sentences ending in a full stop
.RB ( "Print \(dqHello, World!\(dq." ),
and the compiler resolves every abstraction at compile time.
.PP
What surprises people is what
.B vox
does
.I not
do. There is no libc: the emitted code makes Linux system calls directly.
There is no garbage collector and no resident runtime system, so an
executable it produces is a flat static binary with no interpreter and no
shared\-library dependencies unless you ask for them. There is no standard
library either \(em the compiler builds and runs with
.I /usr/include/vox
empty. The only thing the compiler ships is
.IR coreasm ,
a tree of NASM macros the emitted assembly
.BR %include s;
see
.B ENVIRONMENT
for how it is found.
.PP
This page documents the
.I program.
The language itself \(em its grammar, types, and standard vocabulary \(em is
specified in LANGUAGE.md, at
.UR https://vox\-lang.dev/docs/
.UE .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-asm
Write the generated assembly to
.I <stem>.asm
and stop there: nothing is assembled and nothing is linked, and
.BR nasm (1)
and
.BR ld (1)
are never invoked. Use it to read what the compiler produced, or to hand the
assembly to another toolchain.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-asm
Leave
.I <stem>.asm
on disk after a successful assemble\-and\-link, instead of deleting it. It
also applies to a build that fails after code generation, so the assembly
that
.BR nasm (1)
rejected is still there to look at.
.TP
.B \-\-run
Run the executable immediately after linking it.
.B vox
then exits with the program's own exit status, so a Vox program's exit code
survives the compile\-and\-run in one step. Rejected with
.BR \-\-shared ,
which produces a library that cannot be executed.
.TP
.B \-\-shared
Build a position\-independent shared library
.RI ( .so )
instead of an executable, and write a companion
.I .lib
interface file beside it describing the exported functions and their types.
This is the only mode that accepts several source files: they are combined
into one compilation unit and linked in a single step, so the
.I coreasm
runtime is emitted once and shared by every library in the
.IR .so .
.TP
.BI \-\-link " libs"
Put already\-built shared libraries on the link line, named by soname
.I stem
\(em the part between
.B lib
and
.BR .so ,
so
.I libmath.so
is
.BR "\-\-link math" .
Several are comma\-separated. Linking anything this way makes the output a
dynamic executable: the compiler adds the loader
.RI ( /lib64/ld\-linux\-x86\-64.so.2 )
and an
.B \-rpath
for each
.BR \-\-lib\-path .
It does not teach the compiler the library's function signatures \(em that is
what
.B see
of a
.I .lib
does \(em so it is for a
.I .so
with no
.IR .lib :
foreign, or hand\-built.
.TP
.BI \-\-lib\-path " paths"
Additional directories, comma\-separated, to search for libraries. A
.I .lib
named by a
.B see
statement is looked for in the containing file's directory first and then in
each of these; the
.I .so
its
.B Location
names is looked for beside the
.I .lib
first and then in each of these. They are also passed to the linker as
.B \-L
and recorded as
.B \-rpath
entries. They are
.I not
consulted for a
.B see
of a
.I .vox
source file.
.TP
.BI \-\-target " arch"
Select the target architecture. This names the
.I coreasm
subdirectory the emitted assembly includes and the dialect it is written in.
.B x86_64
is the default and the only complete target;
.I coreasm/aarch64
and
.I coreasm/Win64
carry a partial core only, and the link step is x86\-64 specific.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Name the output file. Without it an executable takes the first source file's
stem
.RI ( hello.vox
becomes
.IR hello )
and a
.B \-\-shared
build becomes
.IR lib<stem>.so .
The companion
.I .lib
of a shared build is derived from this name, not from the source.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Report each stage as it happens \(em which file is being compiled, where the
assembly was written, when
.BR nasm (1)
and
.BR ld (1)
run, and whether the intermediate assembly was kept or removed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit successfully.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the compiler version and exit successfully.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VOX_CORE_PATH
Directory holding the
.I coreasm
runtime, or its parent \(em both are accepted. Highest priority of every
source below, and the one to set when working on the compiler or on
.I coreasm
itself.
.TP
.B EC_CORE_PATH
Deprecated alias for
.BR VOX_CORE_PATH ,
left working for shell profiles and CI written before the
.BR ec " \(-> " vox
rename.
.B VOX_CORE_PATH
wins when both are set; when only this one is found the compiler prints a
one\-line note pointing at the new name.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Locate the per\-user configuration file described under
.BR FILES .
.TP
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " HOME
Locate the per\-user cache the compiler writes its embedded copy of
.I coreasm
into, as the last resort described below.
.
.SS Finding coreasm
The compiler needs
.I coreasm
on every compile that assembles anything, and searches for it in this order,
taking the first that exists:
.IP 1. 4
.B VOX_CORE_PATH
.RB ( EC_CORE_PATH
as a deprecated alias).
.IP 2. 4
.IR ~/.config/vox/config ,
the
.B core_path=
line
.RI ( ~/.config/ec/config
as a deprecated alias).
.IP 3. 4
.IR /usr/local/share/vox/coreasm ,
then
.IR /usr/share/vox/coreasm ,
then
.IR /opt/vox/coreasm .
.IP 4. 4
A
.I coreasm
directory beside the
.B vox
executable, or in any directory above it \(em for portable installs.
.IP 5. 4
.I ./coreasm
in the current working directory.
.IP 6. 4
The copy carried inside the binary itself, written out to
.I ~/.cache/vox/<version>/coreasm
on first use. This is last on purpose, so every choice above still wins; it
exists because
.B cargo install
copies only the binary and leaves the crate's
.I coreasm
behind.
.PP
Step 3 is a trap worth knowing if you develop on the compiler with a package
installed: with
.B VOX_CORE_PATH
unset, the installed
.I /usr/share/vox/coreasm
wins over the
.I coreasm
in your source tree, even when you run
.I ./target/release/vox
from inside it. Edits to
.I coreasm/*/*.asm
then appear to do nothing. Export
.B VOX_CORE_PATH="$PWD/coreasm"
whenever you test a
.I coreasm
change by hand.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/vox/coreasm
The NASM runtime macros installed by the package.
.TP
.I ~/.config/vox/config
Per\-user configuration. One
.B core_path=<path>
line, naming either the
.I coreasm
directory or its parent. Lines starting with
.B #
are comments.
.I ~/.config/ec/config
is read as a deprecated alias, with the
.B vox
file winning when both exist.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/vox/<version>/coreasm
Where the compiler materialises its embedded copy of
.I coreasm
on first use. Keyed by compiler version, so two installed versions never
share one tree.
.TP
.I /usr/include/vox/*.lib
Interface files of installed Vox libraries, and
.I /usr/lib64/lib*.so
their shared objects. Neither is needed to build or run the compiler; they
ship separately, in
.BR vox\-libs .
.TP
.I <stem>.asm
Generated assembly, written next to where the compiler runs. Removed after a
successful link unless
.B \-\-keep\-asm
was given; kept as the whole output of
.BR \-\-emit\-asm .
.TP
.I <stem>.o
The assembled object file. Always removed once the link succeeds.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Compile and run a program in one step:
.PP
.EX
$ cat hello.vox
Print "Hello, World!".
$ vox hello.vox \-\-run
Hello, World!
.EE
.PP
Build an executable under a chosen name, and read the assembly the compiler
would have handed to
.BR nasm (1)
\(em the
.B %include
lines are how it reaches
.IR coreasm :
.PP
.EX
$ vox hello.vox \-o greeter
$ ./greeter
Hello, World!
$ vox hello.vox \-\-emit\-asm
$ grep '%include' hello.asm
%include "coreasm/x86_64/core.asm"
%include "coreasm/x86_64/io.asm"
%include "coreasm/x86_64/string.asm"
.EE
.PP
Build a shared library and consume it from Vox. The
.B see
statement resolves the
.IR .lib ,
type\-checks the call against it, and links the
.I .so
\(em there is no
.B \-\-link
on the consumer's command line:
.PP
.EX
$ cat mathkit_lib.vox
Library mathkit version "1.0".

To 'add two numbers' with a number called aa and a number called b. Return a number, aa add b.
$ vox mathkit_lib.vox \-\-shared \-o libmathkit.so
$ cat consumer.vox
see mathkit version "1.0" from "./libmathkit.lib".

a number called sum is 'add two numbers' of 3 and 4.
Print the sum.
$ vox consumer.vox \-o consumer \-\-run
7
.EE
.PP
Link an executable against a
.I .so
that has no
.I .lib
\(em named by soname stem, found through a search path:
.PP
.EX
$ vox main.vox \-\-link mathkit \-\-lib\-path ./libs \-o main
.EE
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested work completed: the program was compiled, or
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-\-version
was printed.
.TP
.B 1
The compiler refused or failed \(em no source file was given, a source file
could not be read, the program did not parse or did not type\-check, several
sources were given without
.BR \-\-shared ,
or
.BR nasm (1)
or
.BR ld (1)
failed. Diagnostics go to standard error.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-run ,
a successful compile is followed by the program itself, and
.B vox
exits with
.I that
program's status rather than its own.
.
